  "worked_solution": "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226336/assets/85730909004322c2c8b6d065ff1cdb4b0662830007bd5aa14c8c8c4bda798206.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\pi ]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =0$ to $\\theta =\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$",
